<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro" name="circe_cable_spool">

    <!--
      CIRCE tether spool: a motor-driven 6 in Cat6 spool in a two-post pillow-block
      frame, bolted to the rear of the chassis deck.

      Six STLs exported from one SolidWorks assembly. They share a single assembly
      coordinate system, so every part is placed with the SAME transform and none of
      them needed re-origining by hand. Do not "tidy" the meshes by re-centring them
      individually - that is exactly what makes this file short.

      *** MESH FRAME (derived from the geometry, not guessed) ***
        +X is the spool rotation axis.  The drum's inertia about X is 0.0047 against
                                       0.0088 transverse - the 1:2 ratio of a solid of
                                       revolution. The drum's and the motor's centres
                                       of mass both land on the line y=139.700,
                                       z=132.807, which fixes the axis independently.
        +Y is up.                      Both end blocks have vertices at exactly y=0 and
                                       nothing below it: that plane is the machined
                                       mounting face. The axis sits 139.700 mm (5.5 in)
                                       above it.
        +Z is the remaining horizontal.
        Units are MILLIMETRES - the assembly measures 278.1 x 254.0 x 228.6 mm, i.e.
        10.95 x 10.00 x 9.00 in. Hence scale="0.001 0.001 0.001" on every mesh.

      Mapping that to ROS (x forward, y left, z up) with the axis ACROSS the rover
      gives rpy = (pi/2, 0, pi/2), i.e. mesh (x,y,z) -> ros (z,x,y). The cable then
      pays off the drum straight backwards as the rover drives forwards, so no
      level-wind is needed.

      *** THIS OCCLUDES THE LIDAR, AND THAT IS WHY THE REAR MASK EXISTS. ***
      The A1's scan plane is at chassis z=0.170. The deck top is at 0.063 and this
      assembly is 0.254 tall, so it reaches z=0.317 - a solid wall straight through the
      scan plane. Measured as an exact mesh/plane cross-section at the mount position
      below, it fills bearings -25.71 .. +25.72 deg in lidar_link at 0.242 .. 0.456 m.
      That is outside the A1's 0.15 m dead zone, so without intervention it reads as a
      wall a quarter-metre behind the rover, to both Nav2 and slam_toolbox.

      circe_perception/config/scan_sector_filter.yaml deletes a +/-30 deg rear sector
      upstream of /scan, which covers this with 4.28 deg to spare each side. That is
      NOT much: the mask was +/-90 until a cold-start Nav2 failure forced it down, and
      the spool is now the thing setting its lower bound. If the spool moves, re-measure
      the sector and confirm it still fits.
    -->

    <!--
      Re-declared so this file stays independently expandable, matching every other
      accessory here. See the sim_mode note in circe_rover.urdf.xacro.
    -->
    <xacro:arg name="sim_mode" default="false" />

    <!--
      ============================ MOUNT POSE ON THE ROVER =======================
      *** MEASURE BEFORE DRIVING. ***

      *** THE MESH AND THE NAV FOOTPRINT DISAGREE ABOUT WHERE THE DECK ENDS. ***
      flipper_chassis.dae runs back to x = -0.348, but circe_nav/config/nav2_params.yaml
      declares the footprint as x = +/-0.27305 - a 75 mm difference, because the mesh is
      the FLIPPER chassis and includes arms this rover does not carry.

      RESOLVED IN FAVOUR OF THE FOOTPRINT. spool_mount_x = -0.1588 is the furthest aft
      the drum fits inside the declared footprint: it puts the drum's rear tangent at
      x = -0.27308, flush with the footprint's rear edge to within 0.03 mm (and well
      inside footprint_padding either way). Mounting to the mesh's rear edge instead
      would need -0.2337, but if the real deck ends where the footprint says it does
      there would be nothing there to bolt to.

      The cost of being 75 mm further forward is paid to the lidar: the spool sits
      closer to the A1, so it subtends a WIDER blind sector - measured -25.71 .. +25.72
      deg here, against -20.64 .. +20.65 deg at -0.2337, and its nearest return closes
      from 0.314 m to 0.242 m. Still inside the +/-30 deg rear mask in
      circe_perception/config/scan_sector_filter.yaml, but with only 4.28 deg of margin
      each side rather than the 9.35 the pre-move sector had - this move used up half
      the headroom. The measured figures quoted in that file track THIS value. Move the
      spool again and re-measure them both.

      spool_deck_z is the top face of flipper_chassis.dae. It is the same measured
      figure as chassis_top_z in rplidar_a1.urdf.xacro, DELIBERATELY duplicated rather
      than referenced: the include order in circe_rover.urdf.xacro is already
      load-bearing for the lidar -> mast -> camera chain and this file has no reason
      to join it. If the deck is ever remeasured, change both.
    -->
    <xacro:property name="spool_mount_x" value="-0.1588" />
    <xacro:property name="spool_mount_y" value="0.0" />
    <xacro:property name="spool_deck_z"  value="0.0630" />

    <!--
      ============================ MESH PLACEMENT ================================
      One rotation and two translations, shared by all six meshes.

      spool_mount_mesh_xyz is -R * (145.182, 0, 132.807) mm: the centre of the base
      footprint, on the mounting plane. So spool_mount_link's origin sits exactly
      where the assembly touches the deck, and spool_mount_z needs no fudge factor.

      spool_drum_mesh_xyz is the same point raised to the rotation axis, so
      spool_drum_link's origin lies ON the axis and the joint below is a pure
      translation of spool_axis_height with no rotation. That is the whole reason the
      two datums are picked this way.
    -->
    <xacro:property name="spool_mesh_rpy"       value="1.5707963 0 1.5707963" />
    <xacro:property name="spool_mount_mesh_xyz" value="-0.132807 -0.145182 0" />
    <xacro:property name="spool_drum_mesh_xyz"  value="-0.132807 -0.145182 -0.139700" />
    <xacro:property name="spool_axis_height"    value="0.139700" />

    <!--
      ============================ MASS ==========================================
      Mesh volumes are exact (divergence-theorem integration over the closed
      triangle soup): drum 1280.9 cm3, the five static parts 2312.4 cm3. Multiplied
      by 1240 kg/m3 for solid PLA/PETG.

      *** BOTH FIGURES ARE UPPER-MIDDLE ESTIMATES, FOR OPPOSITE REASONS. ***
      A printed part at 20-40 % infill weighs well under the solid figure, so the
      blocks and drum are over-counted. The gearmotor is NOT plastic: 82.8 cm3 of
      steel and copper is roughly 0.5 kg, not the 0.103 kg counted here, so the mount
      is under-counted by ~0.4 kg. They partly cancel. Weigh the real thing.

      The inertia coefficients below are the mesh-derived second moments per
      kilogram, in m2, about each link's own centre of mass and already rotated into
      the link frame. Inertia is written as mass * coefficient so that correcting a
      mass keeps the tensor consistent instead of silently desyncing it - which is
      the usual way these end up wrong.
    -->
    <xacro:property name="spool_drum_mass"  value="1.588" />
    <xacro:property name="spool_mount_mass" value="2.867" />


    <!--
      ============================ STATIC FRAME ==================================
      Both end blocks, both top caps, and the gearmotor. Everything that does not turn.
    -->
    <link name="spool_mount_link">

        <xacro:macro name="spool_static_part" params="mesh">
            <visual>
                <origin xyz="${spool_mount_mesh_xyz}" rpy="${spool_mesh_rpy}"/>
                <geometry>
                    <mesh filename="package://circe_description/meshes/${mesh}"
                          scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
                </geometry>
            </visual>
        </xacro:macro>

        <xacro:spool_static_part mesh="spool_motor_end_block.stl" />
        <xacro:spool_static_part mesh="spool_motor_end_block_top.stl" />
        <xacro:spool_static_part mesh="spool_free_end_block.stl" />
        <xacro:spool_static_part mesh="spool_free_end_block_top.stl" />
        <xacro:spool_static_part mesh="spool_motor.stl" />

        <!--
          Collision is two boxes, not the meshes. The blocks are prismatic, so a box
          is a tight fit rather than a lazy one, and it keeps the broadphase cheap.
          The bounds are the real per-part extents in this frame, so the boxes are
          conservative exactly where the parts have pockets and nowhere else.

          The gearmotor gets no collision volume: it lives between the two blocks,
          inside the drum's swept cylinder, and can never be the first thing hit.
        -->
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 -0.1149 0.1053" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <box size="0.1908 0.0597 0.2106"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>

        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0.1073 0.1053" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <box size="0.1908 0.0445 0.2106"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>

        <!--
          iyz is kept because it is ~11 % of iyy: the motor end is heavier and taller
          than the free end, so the assembly genuinely is not principal-axis aligned.
          ixy and ixz are ~4e-06 and dropped.
        -->
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="0 -0.0124 0.0931" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="${spool_mount_mass}" />
            <inertia ixx="${spool_mount_mass * 0.0151695}"
                     iyy="${spool_mount_mass * 0.0054529}"
                     izz="${spool_mount_mass * 0.0137283}"
                     ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0"
                     iyz="${spool_mount_mass * 0.00058379}" />
        </inertial>
    </link>

    <joint name="spool_mount_to_chassis" type="fixed">
        <parent link="chassis_link"/>
        <child link="spool_mount_link"/>
        <origin xyz="${spool_mount_x} ${spool_mount_y} ${spool_deck_z}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
    </joint>


    <!--
      ============================ DRUM ==========================================
      The only part that turns. Its link origin is on the rotation axis, so the joint
      is a pure z translation and the axis is a clean unit vector.
    -->
    <link name="spool_drum_link">
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="${spool_drum_mesh_xyz}" rpy="${spool_mesh_rpy}"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="package://circe_description/meshes/spool_drum.stl"
                      scale="0.001 0.001 0.001"/>
            </geometry>
        </visual>

        <!--
          A cylinder at the FLANGE diameter, not the mesh. Radius 0.1143 (4.5 in) and
          length 0.22352 (8.8 in) are the drum's exact extents. Collision against a
          spool should be against the flanges anyway - the groove between them is not
          somewhere another body can get to - and a spinning concave collision mesh is
          the kind of thing that makes gz-sim's contact solver misbehave.

          Offset +0.0216 in y because the drum is not centred on the mount datum; the
          datum is the centre of the bolt-down footprint, which the motor end skews.
        -->
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0.0216 0" rpy="1.5707963 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <cylinder radius="0.1143" length="0.22352"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>

        <!--
          iyy is the axial moment and is half of ixx/izz, as it must be for a solid of
          revolution. If a future edit makes those three drift out of that ratio, the
          mesh transform is wrong. Products of inertia are all below 2e-05 and dropped.
        -->
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="0 0.0051 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="${spool_drum_mass}" />
            <inertia ixx="${spool_drum_mass * 0.0058465}"
                     iyy="${spool_drum_mass * 0.0031109}"
                     izz="${spool_drum_mass * 0.0058509}"
                     ixy="0.0" ixz="0.0" iyz="0.0" />
        </inertial>
    </link>

    <!--
      Continuous, because a tether spool has no end stops.

      Nothing commands this joint yet. On hardware joint_state_publisher
      (circe_bringup/launch/rover.launch.py) publishes it at 0 like the wheel joints,
      so TF below spool_drum_link stays connected; in sim the gz JointStatePublisher
      system reports it. Neither DRIVES it - the damping and friction below are what
      stop an uncommanded free joint from windmilling under the rover's own
      accelerations and shaking the model. Replace them with a real actuator when the
      payout controller lands.
    -->
    <joint name="spool_drum_to_mount" type="continuous">
        <parent link="spool_mount_link"/>
        <child link="spool_drum_link"/>
        <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
        <origin xyz="0 0 ${spool_axis_height}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
        <dynamics damping="0.05" friction="0.10"/>
    </joint>

</robot>
